1 OCCU Överste i 30-åriga kriget
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Tomas Thomson, natural. Thomson, till Vrana i Sköllersta socken, Örebro län. Född i Skottland. Skall hava kommit i svensk tjänst redan 1595. Var 1622 kapten och kompanichef vid Närkes och Värmlands regemente. Kalla
2 CONC s överkvartermästare därst. 1623. Var 1626 major därst. och 1629 överstelöjtnant: överste och chef för regementet 1631 och förde detsamma under trettioåriga kriget. Var 1638 kommendant i Hagelberg i Tyskland och blev där tillfånga
2 CONC tagen av de kejserliga, men utlöste sig mot ett belopp av 1,000 riksdaler för egen del och 500 riksdaler för några andra fångar. Natural. svensk adelsman 1642 (introd. s. å. under nr 272, vilket nr sedan ändrats till 299). Död 164
2 CONC 3 efter 26/7. Han erhöll 1629-04-25 av konung Gustaf II Adolf Vrana gård och två gårdar i Munkevrak i Stora Mellösa socken, Örebro län samt fick 1642-04-15 som ersättning för ovann. belopp och innestående lön åtta hemman i Sköller
2 CONC sta och Askers socknar. 'Hade med sin fru femton söner, av vilka tretton dogo unga eller ogifta.' Gift med Catharina Mauritzdotter Murray, född omkr. 1586, levde änka ännu i sept. 1666, men var död 1669-02-00 Hon fick 1645-07-10 k
2 CONC onfirmation på Vrana och Munkevrak.
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMSON, THOMAS [SSNE 1648]
2 CONT
2 CONT Surname
2 CONT     THOMSON, THOMSSON First name
2 CONT     THOMAS Title/rank
2 CONT     COLONEL Nationality
2 CONT     SCOT Social status
2 CONT     NOBILITY
2 CONT
2 CONT Text source
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Thomson was born in Scotland and probably entered Swedish service in 1595. By 1622 he was a captain and company chief for the Närke and Värmland regiment. In 1623 he became 'over' quartermaster and by 1626 he became a major
2 CONC . By 1630/31 (one source erroneously notes it as 1634) Thomson was elevated to colonel and chief of the Närke and Värmland regiment, a position he maintained until 1643. With them he served in the 6th Brigade of Leslie's Army of t
2 CONC he Weser at the Battle of Wittstock in September 1636. In 1638 his fortunes changed. While he was commandant at Havelberg in Germany he was captured by the Imperialist forces. However Thomson got himself released for a ransom of 1
2 CONC 000 riksdaler for himself and 500 riksdaler for some fellow prisoners. He was ennobled in Sweden in 1642, and introduced into the Riddarhus (house of nobility in Stockholm) under the number 272 (another source says 299). He only h
2 CONC eld his noble title of Vrana briefly as he died on 26/07/1643 (although another source says 1645). Thomas married Catharina Mauritzdotter Murray [SSNE 6255](1586-1669) with whom he had 15 children, many of whom were also in Swedis
2 CONC h military service in their father's regiment including Alexander [SSNE 3664] and Patrick [SSNE 3670]. Monro mentions a Scottish Colonel Thomson commanding a Swedish regiment in 1632 which really has to be this man. However he sta
2 CONC ted in the book (written in 1637) that the colonel had since died. This was an error and in fact Thomson went on to serve for many years after. On 15 April 1642, Thomson received and award of eight hemman in Narke as recognition o
2 CONC f his service. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Riksarkivets ämnessamlingar. Personhistoria https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0074122_00208#?c=&m=&s=&cv=207&xywh=2611%2C331%2C4018%2C2203
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned in the correspondence of his son in  https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0074122_00104#?c=&m=&s=&cv=103&xywh=2837%2C2194%2C3346%2C1834
2 CONT
2 CONT Also here: https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0074122_00137#?c=&m=&s=&cv=136&xywh=2584%2C2292%2C4019%2C2203
2 CONT
2 CONT Notarial copies of letters and other matters to do with him are found in the correspondence of his grandson, also Thomas Thomson [SSNE 8318]. https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0074122_00215#?c=&m=&s=&cv=214&xywh=3346%2C2355%
2 CONC 2C2787%2C1528
2 CONT
2 CONT KRA Muster Roll,1618/12; 1619/11 and through until 1643; C.O. Nordensvan, (ed.), Värmlands Regementes [Närkes och Värmlands Regementes] Historia: Andra Delen, Personal Historia (Stockholm, 1911), 26; R. Monro, His Expedition with 
2 CONC a worthy Scots Regiment called Mac-Keyes (2 vols., London, 1637), II, The List of the Scottish Officers in Chiefe; Several letters can be found in 'Krigskollegium Kancelliet; Adressatregistratur till Krigskollegiets Registratur 16
2 CONC 31-1654' including those from 3/4/1631 to 16/10/1648; Swedish Riksarkiv, Militära chefer i svenska arméen och deras skrivelser; Svenska Adelns Ättartavlor, vol 8, pp.264-265; F. Fryxell, Wermelandia Nobilitata, list compiled at La
2 CONC ndsarkivet, Göteborg, (1945) p.31; H. Marryat, One Year in Sweden, including a visit to the isle of Gotland (London, 1862), p.499; H.O. Prytz, Historiska Upplysningar om svenska och norska armeernas regementer och kårer jemte flot
2 CONC torna, II, (Stockholm, 1868), p.610. We thank Dr Bernd Warlich for corrections to this article and suggesting the following additional reference: Schröer, Fritz, Das Havelland im Dreißigjährigen Krieg. Ein Beitrag zur Geschichte d
2 CONC er Mark Brandenburg. Mitteldeutsche Forschungen vol. 37. Köln/Graz 1966 p. 294, 182.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two letters from his wife (named as Catharina Mauritz) were sent to Axel Oxenstierna. One is dated 1636, the other is undated. See SRA, Oxenstiernska samlingen E740. Catharina Moritz to Axel Oxenstierna, 1x December 1636 and 1x un
2 CONC dated. Service record
2 CONT
2 CONT SWEDEN,
2 CONT     Arrived 1595-01-01, as SOLDIER
2 CONT     Departed 1617-01-01, as ENSIGN
2 CONT     Capacity OFFICER, purpose MILITARY SWEDEN, SODERMANLAN, NIEROTHS (later PATRICK RUTHVEN) SMALAND
2 CONT     Arrived 1617-01-01, as ENSIGN
2 CONT     Capacity OFFICER, purpose MILITARY SWEDEN, ALEXANDER LESLIE, THOMAS THOMSON'S NARKE OCH VARMLAND; HAVELBERG
2 CONT     Arrived 1623-01-01, as CAPTAIN
2 CONT     Departed 1643-06-27, as COLONEL IN CHIEF
2 CONT     Capacity OFFICER AND COMMANDANT, purpose MILITARY
